{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789050306,"nonce":"Lw06mh+vFchVZET1W8vO8otJFtE=","title":"The sys suite's AOT binary aborts at teardown, about one CI run in three","message":"sys under ash:aot passes all 1118 assertions and then raises SIGABRT.\n\n    CRASH ash:aot bin/hl/sys.hl 5162ms  === CRASH: SIGABRT (signal 6) fault_addr=0x3e90000663a pc=0x7f50fec9ec0c\n\nThe row still records assertions=1118/1118 errors=0, so every test passed and the process died on the way out.\n\n**Rate.** Three CI runs: pass, CRASH (34480094810), pass. Six consecutive passes on the NUC through the harness, plus more since. So it is intermittent rather than environmental, and rare.\n\nfault_addr 0x3e90000663a is not an address: for a SIGABRT raised through abort() the kernel puts (uid\u003c\u003c32 | pid) in si_addr -- 0x3e9 is uid 1001 and 0x663a the pid. It says nothing about where.\n\n**Why the cause is unknown.** glibc prints its reason immediately before aborting -- `free(): invalid pointer`, `malloc(): corrupted top size`, a failed assertion -- and the harness kept only ash's own crash banner and threw the rest away. Fixed in 70c4b2d: the crash detail now carries the lines preceding the banner. The next occurrence will name it. Do not guess at the GC or at atexit ordering until it has.\n\nNot reproducible on the NUC so far, but note that machine runs glibc 2.43 against CI's 2.39 on ubuntu-24.04, and glibc's malloc hardening differs between them; there is no container runtime on the NUC to match it.","files":null}]}